---
// src/components/InBetweenSection1.astro
//
// First "in-between" section — a short breathing-room section you
// asked for, sitting between IntroIslandSection and
// EducationIslandSection, purely visual (no title/eyebrow/text props
// like the island sections have — just a `<slot />` in case you want
// to drop something into it later).
//
// Background: your actual `in_between_section_background_1.webp` image
// (`backgroundImage` prop), via `background-image` + `background-size:
// cover`, same technique every section in this family uses — not a CSS
// recreation. Went through two CSS-gradient attempts first (a radial
// glow, then a tilted linear-gradient) trying to recreate the image's
// look; per your call, simplest and most faithful is to just use the
// real file and layer the dot effect on top of it, so that's what this
// is now. `background-position: bottom` protects the true bottom edge
// (the one that has to match EducationIslandSection's top); `cover`
// guarantees the image always fills the section edge-to-edge with no
// gaps — briefly tried `auto 100%` (height-locked, crop sides only)
// per your request to stop shortening the image's height on wide
// screens, but that left visible white space on screens wider (in
// aspect ratio) than the image itself, which you didn't want either —
// back to `cover`, which only ever crops the minimum needed (height OR
// sides, whichever is required) to avoid that gap.
//
// One more layer on top of that: on a short/wide viewport, `cover`
// still has to crop SOMETHING to avoid a gap, and per your follow-up
// that "something" shouldn't ever be the image's own height either —
// you'd rather scroll than see it shortened. Fixed by adding
// `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` (the image's real ratio) alongside
// `min-height`/`width: 100%` on `.in-between-section` below, so the
// section itself grows taller than one screen on those viewports
// instead of asking `cover` to crop into the height — see the comment
// there.
//
// The dot-grid canvas (`.in-between-stars`): the exact same technique
// as IntroIslandSection.astro's star field — a regular grid of mostly-
// dim, sparsely-bright dots on an offscreen "resting" bitmap (blitted
// each frame), with a small live-updated set of dots near the cursor
// (also including the `rowPhase` fix that keeps this grid's rows lined
// up with IntroIslandSection's, so the seam between them reads as one
// continuous dot field instead of an interrupted one — see the
// `rowPhase` comment on `resize()` below, and the matching one in
// IntroIslandSection.astro)
// easing brighter/bigger and back down — see the top-of-file comment
// there for the full breakdown of why it's built that way. Per your
// request ("the dark part would work the same way as
// IntroIslandSection, with the dots"), it's masked
// (`mask-image`/`-webkit-mask-image`) with a radial gradient anchored
// at the bottom-left corner — opaque (dots fully visible) toward the
// dark top/top-right of the real photo, fading to fully transparent by
// the time it reaches the bright cyan bottom-left corner. That anchor
// point and falloff aren't a guess — they're fit to pixel colors
// actually sampled from your background image (bottom-left corner,
// top-right corner, and points along the diagonal between them), which
// showed close to the same brightness roughly equidistant from that
// corner in every direction: bright cyan right at the corner, fading
// through navy to essentially black by about 70-80% of the way to the
// far corner. That's what keeps the dots confined to "the dark part"
// of the actual photo instead of also showing up (faintly, since
// they're white) over its brighter end.
//
// If you ever swap in a differently-lit background image, the mask's
// anchor/stops on `.in-between-stars` below may need to move to match
// wherever the new image's dark region actually is.
//
// `z-index: 0` on `.in-between-section` (alongside its `position:
// relative`) is required, not decorative — without it, this element
// wouldn't establish its own stacking context, and `.in-between-stars`
// (z-index: -1) would escape past it to be resolved against some
// ancestor's stacking context instead, letting this section's own
// background image paint OVER the canvas instead of under it. This is
// the exact bug that made IntroIslandSection's star grid invisible at
// first — see the fix comment there for the full explanation.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against IntroIslandSection
// above it and EducationIslandSection below it. Still carries
// `.sky-chain` so the gap between them stays collapsed to 0.
//
// Deliberately no `halftone-hover` — same reasoning as
// IntroIslandSection: that page-wide cursor-halftone effect is its own
// dot pattern, and layering it on top of this section's OWN dot canvas
// would just be two dot textures competing visually.
//
// Usage:
//   <InBetweenSection1
//     id="section-in-between-1"
//     backgroundImage="/images/in_between_section_background_1.webp"
//   />
//   <!-- or, if you want to put something in it: -->
//   <InBetweenSection1
//     id="section-in-between-1"
//     backgroundImage="/images/in_between_section_background_1.webp"
//   >
//     <p>Optional content here.</p>
//   </InBetweenSection1>

// New: `backgroundImage` is now an imported src/images/ module
// (an `ImageMetadata` object), not a public/images/ URL string — see
// src/pages/index.astro's frontmatter note for why. This section
// paints it as a CSS `background-image`, not an `<img>`/`<Image />`
// element, so the Image component itself doesn't apply here; instead
// `getImage()` (the same underlying astro:assets pipeline `<Image />`
// uses) runs it through Astro's optimizer at build time and hands
// back a real, already-optimized URL to drop into `url(...)` below.
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro:assets';
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';

interface Props {
  id: string;
  backgroundImage: ImageMetadata;
}

const { id, backgroundImage } = Astro.props;
const optimizedBackgroundImage = await getImage({ src: backgroundImage });
---

<section
  class="in-between-section sky-chain"
  id={id}
  style={`background-image: url(${optimizedBackgroundImage.src});`}
>
  <canvas class="in-between-stars" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

  <div class="content">
    <slot />
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same star-grid technique as IntroIslandSection.astro — see the top-of-file comment there for the full design rationale (resting bitmap + small live-boosted set, ResizeObserver, reduced-motion handling, etc.). Duplicated here rather than shared, matching this codebase's existing convention of each section owning its own copy of whatever script it needs.
  //
  // Wrapped in `astro:page-load` (fires on the initial load AND after
  // every <ClientRouter /> navigation -- see src/layouts/Layout.astro)
  // instead of running at the top level: a language switch replaces
  // this `<canvas>` with a freshly-parsed one, and this module itself
  // only executes once, so without re-running this init on every
  // navigation the star field would go dead (and the ResizeObserver
  // would be left watching a detached canvas) after the first language
  // switch. `cleanupInBetweenStars` tears down the previous navigation's rAF
  // loop, `mousemove`/`mouseout` listeners, ResizeObserver, and pending
  // resize-debounce timer first, so switching languages back and forth
  // all session never piles up duplicates of any of them running at
  // once (`window`/`document` are never recreated by a soft
  // navigation).
  let cleanupInBetweenStars: (() => void) | null = null;

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
    cleanupInBetweenStars?.();

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const { signal } = controller;
    let resizeObserver: ResizeObserver | null = null;
    const rafIdHolder: { get: () => number | null } = { get: () => null };
    const resizeTimerHolder: { get: () => ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null } = {
      get: () => null,
    };

    const canvas = document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>(".in-between-stars");
    const ctx = canvas?.getContext("2d") ?? null;

    if (canvas && ctx) {
      const SPACING = 24;
      const BRIGHT_CHANCE = 0.035;
      const INFLUENCE_RADIUS = 130;
      const EASE = 0.15;
      const MAX_DPR = 2;

      const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
        "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
      ).matches;

      function hash(x: number, y: number) {
        const v = Math.sin(x * 12.9898 + y * 78.233) * 43758.5453;
        return v - Math.floor(v);
      }

      interface Dot {
        x: number;
        y: number;
        isBright: boolean;
        baseAlpha: number;
        baseRadius: number;
        boost: number;
      }

      let dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, MAX_DPR);
      let width = 0;
      let height = 0;
      let cols = 0;
      let rows = 0;
      let rowPhase = 0; // see resize() — kept in this scope so activateNearby() can undo the shift
      let dots: Dot[] = [];
      let restingBitmap: HTMLCanvasElement | null = null;
      const active = new Set<number>();

      let mouseX = -99999;
      let mouseY = -99999;
      let rafId: number | null = null;
      rafIdHolder.get = () => rafId;

      // `rowPhase` shifts every row's y-coordinate so the grid lines up
      // with the PAGE's global 24px rhythm instead of restarting fresh
      // at this canvas's own top edge — see the comment on `resize()`
      // below for why (this is what keeps the dot grid seamless across
      // the boundary from IntroIslandSection, which does the exact same
      // thing). Row indices used for `hash()`/`active` bookkeeping stay
      // plain 0..rows-1 integers regardless — only the drawn `y` value
      // is shifted.
      function buildDots(rowPhase: number) {
        cols = Math.ceil(width / SPACING) + 1;
        rows = Math.ceil((height + rowPhase) / SPACING) + 1;
        dots = [];
        for (let row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
          for (let col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
            const roll = hash(col * 3 + 1, row * 7 + 5);
            const isBright = roll < BRIGHT_CHANCE;
            dots.push({
              x: col * SPACING,
              y: row * SPACING - rowPhase,
              isBright,
              baseAlpha: isBright
                ? 0.75 + hash(col, row + 11) * 0.25
                : 0.08 + hash(col + 11, row) * 0.14,
              baseRadius: isBright ? 1.7 : 1.05,
              boost: 0,
            });
          }
        }
        active.clear();
      }

      function renderResting() {
        const bitmap = document.createElement("canvas");
        bitmap.width = canvas.width;
        bitmap.height = canvas.height;
        const rctx = bitmap.getContext("2d");
        if (!rctx) return;

        rctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
        for (const d of dots) {
          rctx.beginPath();
          rctx.fillStyle = `rgba(255, 255, 255, ${d.baseAlpha.toFixed(3)})`;
          if (d.isBright) {
            rctx.shadowColor = "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85)";
            rctx.shadowBlur = 5;
          } else {
            rctx.shadowBlur = 0;
          }
          rctx.arc(d.x, d.y, d.baseRadius, 0, Math.PI * 2);
          rctx.fill();
        }

        restingBitmap = bitmap;
      }

      function drawFrame() {
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);
        if (restingBitmap) {
          ctx.drawImage(restingBitmap, 0, 0, width, height);
        }

        for (const idx of active) {
          const d = dots[idx];
          if (!d || d.boost <= 0.01) continue;
          const alpha = Math.min(1, d.baseAlpha + d.boost * 0.7);
          const radius = d.baseRadius + d.boost * 1.6;
          ctx.beginPath();
          ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(255, 255, 255, ${alpha.toFixed(3)})`;
          ctx.shadowColor = "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9)";
          ctx.shadowBlur = 4 + d.boost * 8;
          ctx.arc(d.x, d.y, radius, 0, Math.PI * 2);
          ctx.fill();
        }
        ctx.shadowBlur = 0;
      }

      function resize() {
        const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        width = rect.width;
        height = rect.height;
        dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, MAX_DPR);
        canvas.width = Math.round(width * dpr);
        canvas.height = Math.round(height * dpr);
        ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);

        // This canvas's top edge in PAGE (document) coordinates — adding
        // back the current scroll offset cancels it out, so this is the
        // same value regardless of where the page happens to be scrolled
        // when resize() runs. Used only to phase-shift the row grid (see
        // `buildDots()`) so it lines up with the page's global 24px
        // rhythm instead of always starting a fresh row right at this
        // canvas's own top — otherwise, since this section's height is
        // basically never an exact multiple of SPACING, the row spacing
        // right at the seam against IntroIslandSection (which sits flush
        // above this section, no gap) would be off — either squeezed
        // together or gapped wider than the normal 24px rhythm, reading
        // as a visible cut/seam in the dot pattern. Both this section and
        // IntroIslandSection compute this the same way independently, so
        // any two adjacent sections using this technique line up
        // automatically without needing to coordinate.
        const pageTop = rect.top + window.scrollY;
        rowPhase = ((pageTop % SPACING) + SPACING) % SPACING;

        buildDots(rowPhase);
        renderResting();
        drawFrame();
      }

      resize();

      let resizeTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
      resizeTimerHolder.get = () => resizeTimer;
      const ro = new ResizeObserver(() => {
        if (resizeTimer) clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
        resizeTimer = setTimeout(resize, 120);
      });
      ro.observe(canvas);
      resizeObserver = ro;

      if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
        function activateNearby(x: number, y: number) {
          // Row lookups need `+ rowPhase` to undo the shift `buildDots()`
          // applied to each dot's actual `y` (see the comment on
          // `resize()`) — column lookups don't, since x is unshifted.
          const minCol = Math.max(0, Math.floor((x - INFLUENCE_RADIUS) / SPACING));
          const maxCol = Math.min(cols - 1, Math.ceil((x + INFLUENCE_RADIUS) / SPACING));
          const minRow = Math.max(0, Math.floor((y + rowPhase - INFLUENCE_RADIUS) / SPACING));
          const maxRow = Math.min(rows - 1, Math.ceil((y + rowPhase + INFLUENCE_RADIUS) / SPACING));

          for (let row = minRow; row <= maxRow; row++) {
            for (let col = minCol; col <= maxCol; col++) {
              active.add(row * cols + col);
            }
          }
        }

        function ensureLoopRunning() {
          if (rafId === null) {
            rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
          }
        }

        function tick() {
          let anyActive = false;

          for (const idx of active) {
            const d = dots[idx];
            if (!d) {
              active.delete(idx);
              continue;
            }

            const dx = d.x - mouseX;
            const dy = d.y - mouseY;
            const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
            const target = dist < INFLUENCE_RADIUS ? 1 - dist / INFLUENCE_RADIUS : 0;

            d.boost += (target - d.boost) * EASE;

            if (d.boost < 0.01 && target === 0) {
              d.boost = 0;
              active.delete(idx);
            } else {
              anyActive = true;
            }
          }

          drawFrame();
          rafId = anyActive ? requestAnimationFrame(tick) : null;
        }

        window.addEventListener(
          "mousemove",
          (e) => {
            const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
            mouseX = e.clientX - rect.left;
            mouseY = e.clientY - rect.top;
            activateNearby(mouseX, mouseY);
            ensureLoopRunning();
          },
          { signal }
        );

        document.addEventListener(
          "mouseout",
          (e) => {
            const leftWindow = !e.relatedTarget && !(e as any).toElement;
            if (leftWindow) {
              mouseX = -99999;
              mouseY = -99999;
              ensureLoopRunning();
            }
          },
          { signal }
        );
      }
    }

    cleanupInBetweenStars = () => {
      controller.abort();
      resizeObserver?.disconnect();
      const raf = rafIdHolder.get();
      if (raf !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
      const timer = resizeTimerHolder.get();
      if (timer !== null) clearTimeout(timer);
    };
  });
</script>

<style>
  .in-between-section {
    position: relative;
    /* Required alongside `position: relative` — see the top-of-file
       comment for why (`.in-between-stars`'s negative z-index needs
       THIS element to be its own stacking context, or its background
       image paints over the canvas instead of under it). */
    z-index: 0;
    min-height: 100vh;
    /* `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` matches the background image (measured at
       3840x2160) — combined with `width: 100%` and `min-height`, the
       browser uses whichever constraint needs MORE height: on a
       viewport wider-than-16:9 (mainly short/wide windows), the
       aspect-ratio wins and this section grows taller than one
       screen, showing the FULL image at its native ratio with no
       cropping — you scroll to see the rest, per your request,
       instead of `cover` shortening (cropping) the image's height to
       fit a too-short viewport. On a taller-than-16:9 viewport (e.g.
       mobile portrait), `min-height: 100vh` wins instead, so the
       section stays exactly one screen tall and `cover` crops the
       sides only — unchanged from before. `width: 100%` is required
       alongside `aspect-ratio` — without it, on the min-height-wins
       branch the browser would stretch this section's WIDTH to
       satisfy the ratio instead of its height, overflowing past the
       viewport edges. */
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* `cover` — always fills the section edge-to-edge with no gaps,
       cropping whichever dimension needs it least (see the top-of-file
       comment for why this reverted back from `auto 100%`). */
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: bottom;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* Full-bleed, behind everything else in the section — same z-index
     pattern as IntroIslandSection's `.intro-stars`. */
  .in-between-stars {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Fades the dots out toward the bright bottom-left corner of the
       real background photo (see the top-of-file comment for the
       sampled pixel data this is fit to) — opaque toward the dark
       top/top-right, transparent near the bright corner, so the dot
       grid only ever shows over "the dark part" of the actual image. */
    mask-image: radial-gradient(
      ellipse at 0% 100%,
      transparent 0%,
      transparent 28%,
      black 60%
    );
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      ellipse at 0% 100%,
      transparent 0%,
      transparent 28%,
      black 60%
    );
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(36ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }
</style>
